 design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186134114 1071 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$8$** | **$16$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-10$** | **$-8$** | **$-5$** | **$-1$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $10$ | $16$ | $22$ | $30$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multi linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186134114 1074 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$8$** | **$16$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-10$** | **$-8$** | **$-5$** | **$-1$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $10$ | $16$ | $22$ | $30$ |\n\n \n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 16 & 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 22 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186134391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186134391 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\beta =[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186134391 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x + β_{2}x^{2}$ that best fits the data.\n\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & 4^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5^{2}\\end{bmatrix}, β = \\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186134391 1074 "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\nX\\beta =y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186135940 1078 #edb/ref 17592186135944 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186138438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558530 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186138438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558558 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186138439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558559 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186138439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558574 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186138560 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558484 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186138560 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558499 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186138832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186138832 1071 "![](images/d7/d73138fe191ed536299fa6dca679e5948b389d26b4d9e32518eee79123f81f0e.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the measure of $BC^(―)$?" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186138832 1071 "![](images/d7/d73138fe191ed536299fa6dca679e5948b389d26b4d9e32518eee79123f81f0e.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the measure of $\\overset{―}{BC}?$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186138832 1074 "The segments addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 28 & =9+BC+7 \\\\ 28 & =16+BC \\\\ 28-16 & =BC \\\\ BC & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186147565 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558483 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186147570 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558510 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186147570 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558525 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186147571 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558533 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186156687 1078 #edb/ref 17592186156690 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157086 1071 "Given the sets $X = \\{ 2, 5, 7, 8 \\},$ $Y = \\{ 1, 3, 5 \\},$ and $Z = \\{ 2, 4, 6, 8 \\},$ find $X \\cap Y,$ $Y \\cap Z,$ and $Z \\cap X.$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157086 1071 "Given the sets $X = \\{2, 5, 7, 8 \\}$, $Y = \\{1, 3, 5 \\}$, and $Z = \\{2, 4, 6, 8 \\}$, find $X \\cap Y$, $Y \\cap Z$, and $Z \\cap X$." 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157086 1074 "The intersection $X \\cap Y$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $X$ and $Y$. Since $X$ and $Y$ have only the element $5$ in common, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX∩Y &= {2, 5, 7, 8}∩{1, 3, 5} \\\\\n&= {5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection $Y \\cap Z$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $Y$ and $Z$. Since $Y$ and $Z$ have no elements in common, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nY∩Z &= {1, 3, 5}∩{2, 4, 6, 8} \\\\\n&= {} \\\\\n&= ∅\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection $Z \\cap X$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $Z$ and $X$. Since $Z$ and $X$ have only the elements $2$ and $8$ in common, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nZ∩X &= {2, 4, 6, 8}∩{2, 5, 7, 8} \\\\\n&= {2, 8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157086 1074 "The intersection $X \\cap Y$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $X$ and $Y.$ Since $X$ and $Y$ have only the element $5$ in common, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cap Y & =\\{2,5,7,8\\}\\cap \\{1,3,5\\} \\\\  & =\\{5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection $Y \\cap Z$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $Y$ and $Z.$ Since $Y$ and $Z$ have no elements in common, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y\\cap Z & =\\{1,3,5\\}\\cap \\{2,4,6,8\\} \\\\  & =\\{\\} \\\\  & =∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection $Z \\cap X$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $Z$ and $X.$ Since $Z$ and $X$ have only the elements $2$ and $8$ in common, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z\\cap X & =\\{2,4,6,8\\}\\cap \\{2,5,7,8\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157087 1071 "Let $A$ and $B$ be the set of all multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ and the set of all multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$, respectively. What is $A \\cap B$?" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157087 1071 "Let $A$ and $B$ be the set of all multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ and the set of all multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0,$ respectively. What is $A \\cap B?$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157087 1074 "The multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32, 36 \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB = \\{ 6,12,18,24,30,36,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, $A \\cap B$ is the *intersection* of $A$ and $B,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in *both* $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cap B & =\\{4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,36,…\\}\\cap \\{6,12,18,24,30,36,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{12,24,36,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157087 1074 "The multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA = \\{4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32, 36 \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB = \\{6,12,18,24,30,36,\\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nNow, $A \\cap B$ is the *intersection* of $A$ and $B$, which means that we want to find the elements that are in *both* $A$ and $B$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA∩B &= {4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 36, …}∩{6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, …} \\\\\n&= {12, 24, 36, …}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157094 1071 "![](images/dc/dcddc9ee6fe49965ea4f261fcb43c6f7ccd14193910e864b950745ef513e67a6.png)\n\nGiven the sets $X = \\{ 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 \\}$ and $Y = \\{ 1, 2, 4, 8 \\}$ shown above, find $X \\cap Y.$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157094 1071 "![](images/dc/dcddc9ee6fe49965ea4f261fcb43c6f7ccd14193910e864b950745ef513e67a6.png)\n\nGiven the sets $X = \\{1, 2, 3, 5, 8 \\}$ and $Y = \\{1, 2, 4, 8 \\}$ shown above, find $X \\cap Y$." 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157094 1074 "The intersection $X \\cap Y$ consists of all the elements that are in both $X$ and $Y$. To represent $X \\cap Y$, we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $X$ and $Y$.\n\n![](images/37/37243a3d1954c0ae7939c09ad05b6aa12a8dff8a3ae8915bbe2079051466cbcb.png)\n\nThe elements $1$, $2$, and $8$ are in both $X$ and $Y$. Therefore, the intersection of $X$ and $Y$ is\n\n$$\nX \\cap Y = \\{1,2,8 \\}\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157094 1074 "The intersection $X \\cap Y$ consists of all the elements that are in both $X$ and $Y.$ To represent $X \\cap Y,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $X$ and $Y.$\n\n![](images/37/37243a3d1954c0ae7939c09ad05b6aa12a8dff8a3ae8915bbe2079051466cbcb.png)\n\nThe elements $1,$ $2,$ and $8$ are in both $X$ and $Y.$ Therefore, the intersection of $X$ and $Y$ is\n\n$$\nX \\cap Y = \\{ 1,2,8 \\}.\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157144 1071 "Write $\\dfrac{5x^2}{5x^2-2}$ in the form $a + \\dfrac{b}{5x^2-2}$, where $a \\neq 0$. What is the value of $a+b$?" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157144 1071 "Write $\\dfrac{5x^2}{5x^2-2}$ in the form $a + \\dfrac{b}{5x^2-2},$ where $a \\neq 0.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157144 1074 "First, we subtract and add $2$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}-2}=\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2+2}{5{x}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2+2}{5{x}^{2}-2} & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{5{x}^{2}-2}+\\frac{2}{5{x}^{2}-2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{2}{5{x}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=1$ and $b=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\na + b = 1 + 2 = 3.\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157144 1074 "First, we subtract and add $2$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x^{2}}{5x^{2} - 2} = \\frac{5x^{2} - 2 + 2}{5x^{2} - 2}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5x^{2} - 2 + 2}{5x^{2} - 2} &= \\frac{5x^{2} - 2}{5x^{2} - 2} + \\frac{2}{5x^{2} - 2} \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{2}{5x^{2} - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=1$ and $b=2$, and we get\n\n$$\na + b = 1 + 2 = 3\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157145 1071 "Write $\\dfrac{3x^2+5}{3x^2-2x}$ in the form $a + \\dfrac{bx+c}{3x^2-2x}$, where $a \\neq 0$. What is the value of $a+b+c$?" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157145 1071 "Write $\\dfrac{3x^2+5}{3x^2-2x}$ in the form $a + \\dfrac{bx+c}{3x^2-2x},$ where $a \\neq 0.$ What is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157145 1074 "First, we subtract and add $2x$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5}{3{x}^{2}-2x}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+2x+5}{3{x}^{2}-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+2x+5}{3{x}^{2}-2x} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x}{3{x}^{2}-2x}+\\frac{2x+5}{3{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{2x+5}{3{x}^{2}-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5,$ and we get\n\n$$\na + b + c = 1 + 2 + 5 = 8.\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157145 1074 "First, we subtract and add $2x$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x^{2} + 5}{3x^{2} - 2x} = \\frac{3x^{2} - 2x + 2x + 5}{3x^{2} - 2x}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x^{2} - 2x + 2x + 5}{3x^{2} - 2x} &= \\frac{3x^{2} - 2x}{3x^{2} - 2x} + \\frac{2x + 5}{3x^{2} - 2x} \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{2x + 5}{3x^{2} - 2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=1$, $b=2$, and $c=5$, and we get\n\n$$\na + b + c = 1 + 2 + 5 = 8\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157146 1071 "Write $\\dfrac{3x^3}{x^3+4}$ in the form $a + \\dfrac{b}{x^3+4}$, where $a \\neq 0$. What is the value of $a+b$?" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157146 1071 "Write $\\dfrac{3x^3}{x^3+4}$ in the form $a + \\dfrac{b}{x^3+4},$ where $a \\neq 0.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157146 1074 "First, we add and subtract $4$ in parentheses in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x^{3}}{x^{3} + 4} &= (3(x^{3} + 4 - 4))/(x^{3} + 4) \\\\\n&= (3(x^{3} + 4) + 3(-4))/(x^{3} + 4) \\\\\n&= (3(x^{3} + 4) - 12)/(x^{3} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3(x^{3} + 4) - 12)/(x^{3} + 4) &= (3(x^{3} + 4))/(x^{3} + 4) + \\frac{-12}{x^{3} + 4} \\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{-12}{x^{3} + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=3$ and $b=-12$, and we get\n\n$$\na + b = 3 + (-12) = -9\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 false] [17592186157146 1074 "First, we add and subtract $4$ in parentheses in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+4} & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+4-4)}{{x}^{3}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+4)+3(-4)}{{x}^{3}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+4)-12}{{x}^{3}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3({x}^{3}+4)-12}{{x}^{3}+4} & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+4)}{{x}^{3}+4}+\\frac{-12}{{x}^{3}+4} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{-12}{{x}^{3}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=3$ and $b=-12,$ and we get\n\n$$\na + b = 3 + (-12) = -9.\n$$" 13194139536023 17592186045417 true] [17592186157167